# Line endings, pinned so every clone agrees.
#
# Without this file the repo's line endings depend on each machine's core.autocrlf,
# which is a per-installation setting nobody sets deliberately — on Windows Git it
# defaults to true, on macOS to input. Two people can then commit the same file and
# see a whole-file diff.
#
# This records the state that already holds rather than changing it: every text blob
# committed here is already LF. Verified with `git ls-files --eol` — 91 text files
# (89 LF, 2 single-line files with no line ending at all), zero i/crlf, zero i/mixed.
# So adding this renormalises nothing.
#
# `eol=lf` additionally pins the WORKING TREE to LF on Windows, where core.autocrlf=true
# would otherwise check out CRLF. That matters here because deployments are assembled by
# hand-copying the working tree: with this, the copied files are byte-identical no matter
# whose machine they came from.
#
# The working tree was already drifting when this was added: 63 files CRLF, 26 LF, for
# no reason other than which of them git had last re-materialised. In mass-measure-01
# and -02 that exact split made a package unreproducible from its own commit and turned
# verify-package.ps1 red on packages that were in fact correct and live.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit binary, so auto-detection is never the only thing standing between a font
# file and corruption. These match what git already infers today.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.woff2 binary

# The production storyboard. `.gitignore` lists *.pptx, but this file was committed
# before that rule existed and .gitignore does not apply to tracked files — so it is
# still in the tree and still needs protecting from eol conversion.
*.pptx  binary

# SVG is XML — text, not an image format, as far as git is concerned. Stated explicitly
# because it is a large group here (28 files) and because "*.svg binary" is a common
# and silently damaging mistake: it would kill diffs and merges on the illustrations.
*.svg  text eol=lf
